LOGBOOK.md:430:- FINDING: `notes` must be **excluded** from a staleness scan. Sweeping all **402** live board elements for a matrix revision label found two hits — the reviewer's finding, and this task's own `notes`, which the reviewer did not report and which is **not a defect**: append-only progress history where "r5 (2026-07-28.r5) closes all three verdict-04 findings" is a *true* statement. Same reason S1 excludes `revisionLog` from its count scan. The obvious "scan every field" implementation manufactures a failure out of correct history; A1 declares `excludedFields` and R34 fails if a field is ever both checked and excluded. The sweep is also what licenses the stronger claim: **no third live consumer carries a revision label**.
LOGBOOK.md:456:- DECISION: `com.apple.security.application-groups` is now **prohibited on the iOS probe pair** (verdict 04 F3). r4 granted it because the probe "exercises the same shared-container path production depends on" — but `TASK-260715-1jckn0` scopes the probe to the `NETunnelProviderManager` lifecycle and versioned app messaging, its AC3 puts the whole exchange on `sendProviderMessage`, and no shared container appears anywhere. Rule G4 gained **`probeRule`**: a probe inherits **identifiers and profile classes, never entitlements**; if a probe is meant to prove a capability can be *provisioned*, that is a verification deliverable and must be an acceptance criterion **first** — the entitlement follows the criterion, not the reverse.
LOGBOOK.md:460:- SCOPE: `TASK-260728-q5kjta` (Ceremony C1) scope + AC4 corrected on the board (verdict 04 F2): four macOS App IDs not a singular pair, Network Extensions only, **zero** App Group records/capabilities, **no** Keychain mutation (there is no portal record), Mac Development profile class, all four iOS App IDs excluded. A downstream-consequence paragraph in the matrix is not the contract the operator reads.
LOGBOOK.md:474:### 0851 — An NE lifecycle read from the SDK header alone was wrong in the direction that mattered (TASK-260728-7ii1xz, r2)
LOGBOOK.md:475:- ROOT CAUSE: r1 concluded "the app cannot reach a stopped provider" from the macOS 26.5 SDK header for `handleAppMessage:` — *"called by the framework when the container app sends a message to the provider"*. That sentence names **who sends**, not a liveness precondition, and the header for the sending side says nothing about launch at all. Apple's published API reference does: `sendProviderMessage(_:responseHandler:)` — *"If the extension is not running, **it should be launched to handle the message**."* One inference from a header that was silent, and revocation was declared permanently asynchronous, `379cpk` AC4 was declared unsatisfiable, and a 60-second startup budget was spent waiting for a seed. **Generalisable: for Network Extension lifecycle questions the shipped header and the published reference are not interchangeable — the header documents the signature, the reference documents the behaviour.** Check both before designing around an absence.
LOGBOOK.md:477:- FINDING: The reverse dependency stays forbidden and is what still kills the memory-only candidate B. Apple, same page: *"you should not rely on this communication channel with the containing app for basic Tunnel Provider operation (start, stop, etc.)."* App→provider may launch; provider→app may never be assumed. The transport selection (candidate D) therefore did not change — only the lifecycle around it.
LOGBOOK.md:481:- DECISION: A settled decision row must have its **pending scaffolding deleted with it**. The ypo7yo matrix row still carried `resolutionOwner: TASK-260728-7ii1xz` and an `amendmentRule` reading "if the transport gives the provider direct keychain access, amend to `required` with the group literal". The selected transport makes that antecedent **true** while the file-based keychain makes the consequent **false** — so leaving it in place was a live instruction to re-grant the row on precisely the reasoning the decision refutes. The amendment packet now covers all five keys on the row field by field. Worth a validator gate: a `prohibited` row that still names a `resolutionOwner` is a contradiction.
LOGBOOK.md:484:- DECISION: Candidate D selected. Host reads the secret in user context, hands it to the running provider via `sendProviderMessage` → `handleAppMessage`, and the **root provider** persists it in the file-based **System keychain** (`/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`) with a restrictive `SecAccess`. This is exactly Apple DTS's recommendation for this shape (forums/thread/759976, /133933). Rejected: shared Data Protection Keychain group (cannot work), memory-only IPC seed (dies on system start), custom XPC Mach service (needs exception entitlements + makes OC-4 live), root-owned encrypted file (Tailscale's pattern — same security, more code, more entitlements).
LOGBOOK.md:488:- FINDING: **The App Group channel is broken on macOS too**, not just the keychain. `containerURL(forSecurityApplicationGroupIdentifier:)` resolves relative to the caller's home and the root provider's home is `/private/var/root` (verified root-private, `ls` exit 1). `.spec/architecture.md:111` says shared state is "an App Group configuration snapshot and Keychain references" — both halves fail on macOS. The non-secret snapshot must travel in `providerConfiguration`, which the accepted M1 lifecycle contract already does. Directly affects `TASK-260715-3f4lxy`.
LOGBOOK.md:494:### 0755 — An exhaustive entitlement allowlist inherits every entitlement the architecture implies, including ones the matrix only mentioned in prose (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r3)
LOGBOOK.md:503:- ROOT CAUSE: The r1 matrix assigned `packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension` to all four macOS targets unconditionally while authorizing **Mac Development** profiles for the same four App IDs. Apple DTS is explicit that the value follows the distribution channel: the `-systemextension` suffix is for direct Developer ID signing, and "otherwise — including when you distribute the app on the App Store and when signing for development — use the values without that suffix" (forums/thread/800887). Ceremony C1 would have provisioned development profiles against a value development signing never uses, and C1 is a one-shot human node (ADR-028).
LOGBOOK.md:519:- ROOT CAUSE: r1's assertion A10 rejected "any entitlement key outside the matrix union", but the matrix authors only project capabilities. The signing toolchain injects more, so A10 would have failed a legitimately signed development bundle — or forced undocumented local exceptions, which is worse.
LOGBOOK.md:527:- DECISION: The matrix withholds `keychain-access-groups` from `works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel` (`prohibited-pending-decision`) rather than provisioning a right the provider cannot exercise. Created `TASK-260728-7ii1xz` to select the macOS credential transport and return a grant-or-keep-prohibited amendment; it blocks `379cpk`, `1o9wjz`, `29ws8l`, `3f4lxy`. Five credential tasks all assumed the shared-group model and none owned the question of whether it holds.
LOGBOOK.md:534:- FINDING: Probe identifiers had to be in this matrix, not invented later by `1r0fxv`. C1 authorizes "exactly the approved macOS entries"; had the probe been absent, provisioning it would have needed a second human sitting — the thing ADR-028 forbids.
LOGBOOK.md:535:- VERIFICATION: `validate_matrix.py` runs 18 rules / 247 checks over the JSON contract, exit 0; nine deliberate mutations (broken containment, legacy-prefixed identifier, wrong NE value, granted pending-keychain row, unprefixed macOS group, collapsed probe group, C1-authorized distribution profile, iOS marked provisioned, sysex-install on the provider) each fail closed with the expected rule.
LOGBOOK.md:553:- ANOMALY: Round 2 removed `1u2vpc ← 2ayxqn`, which scheduled the libssh2 functional/rekey matrix **before** Gate P0 existed while the matrix scope still required a Gate-P0 provider smoke on this Mac. Restored rather than re-scoped, so no Apple-target row is weakened. Consequence: M0 engine selection (`1gjxer`) correctly lands after P0.
LOGBOOK.md:575:- FINDING: The root cause of the M1 deadlock was a contract/integration inversion, not a milestone-ordering mistake. `TASK-260715-1pn983` (binding memory ledger) was blocked by `30ugfm` (M1 routing integration) and `z37ay7` (M2 UDP enforcement) — both are work the ledger is supposed to *govern*. Removing those two edges and adding `z37ay7 blockedBy 1pn983` broke two cycles and kept the reviewer-added `3miqh4 ← 1pn983` accountability gate intact; `productionAuthorization` stays `false`.
LOGBOOK.md:579:- VERIFICATION: `task-board validate` → valid, 0 issues (was 46). Independent cycle traversal → 0. No source tree change: `git status --porcelain -- Sources Tests Protocol relay scripts config Package.swift Makefile` is empty. Invariant scan confirms host-key-before-auth, Keychain-only secrets, fail-closed DNS, bounded memory, public packetFlow bridge, rootless exec/stdio relay, and signed/notarized release all survive the edit. Evidence: `.task-board/.resources/TASK-260728-3a2dnr/`.
LOGBOOK.md:631:- DECISION: Protocol v1 fixes synthetic workload generation, ten deterministic seeds, controlled impairment profiles, warmup/steady/cooldown schedules, sampling, paired bootstrap and practical-effect rules, non-tradeable byte/DNS/route/resource/memory/failure/drop/cleanup gates, content-addressed raw references, capture authorization, privacy classes, and immutable pass/red/unavailable/invalid outcomes before tuning begins. macOS named-device rows are current; iPhone slots are schema-compatible deferred rows and are not guessed from Mac evidence.
LOGBOOK.md:650:- SECURITY: Manifest, identity, and executable reads use one regular-file `O_NOFOLLOW` descriptor; executable size and SHA-256 come from the same open descriptor, removing the prior path-swap window. `test-relay-shell-artifacts.sh` captures exact stdout/stderr in a mode-0700/umask-077 temporary directory and invokes the comparator before stdio smoke for native Darwin arm64 and Rosetta amd64.
LOGBOOK.md:672:- FINDING: The typed `queueSaturated` / `datagramTooLarge` path preserves the HEV descriptor and association ID, but `HEVUDPDatagramAdapter.observeNonterminalRelayError` validates ownership through `ClientUDPAssociationRegistry.resolveRemoteDatagram`. That API is explicitly datagram activity: it cancels the existing timer, advances the idle deadline, schedules a replacement timer, and increments `activityUpdates`. A relay error-only pressure observation therefore prolongs association lifetime, contrary to the rework contract that nonterminal error accounting be an aggregate observation only.
LOGBOOK.md:678:- LIFECYCLE: Deterministic fake-relay regressions prove both nonterminal signals permit later outbound and inbound datagrams on the exact same association ID, followed by an independent peer close/ack. Finite and unknown terminal errors in error-first and close-first orderings produce one descriptor close, one registry release, one relay close, and zero retained queue bytes per owner.
LOGBOOK.md:683:- RESOURCE: Per-association and aggregate input/output reservations use the accepted envelope charge, drop newest on pressure, and keep oversized HEV ingress on a bounded structural-header-plus-skip path. One worker/read loop and one serialized writer exist per admitted private connection; cancellation, timeout, close, error, expiry, and session loss return queues and registry ownership to baseline.
LOGBOOK.md:689:- ADDRESS: Resolver results with a non-empty IPv6 zone are discarded before unmapping, accepted-result byte credit, family/socket admission, or send. An all-scoped result set closes its original bounded reservation with zero descriptors/sends and zero retained scheduler/registry resources; a later in-cap unzoned IPv6 result is selected byte-exactly without zone rewriting. The system socket seam also rejects zoned IPv6 defensively.
LOGBOOK.md:699:- LIFECYCLE: Registry retirement cancels an incarnation-local context on close, idle expiry, generation replacement, session loss, process shutdown, or parent cancellation. Active and queued jobs recheck that context before resolver/socket work, and token-scoped socket admission prevents a paused old lookup from reopening or sending through a same-generation reused ID. Deterministic barriers restore job, queue, name, payload, result, completion, worker, association, timer, socket, and descriptor counts to their declared baselines.
LOGBOOK.md:700:- ADDRESS/PRECEDENCE: IPv4-mapped IPv6 is rejected before association or descriptor admission. `MSG_TRUNC` now returns before sockaddr conversion/materialization, so a truncated reply with an otherwise unsupported/scoped source remains a counted nonterminal oversize drop. Numeric/domain and reply-source byte preservation, readiness-only retry, round-robin budgets, and privacy-safe diagnostics remain unchanged.
LOGBOOK.md:704:### 1645 — Relay UDP I/O review rejects resolver lifecycle ordering (TASK-260715-3xpc6b)
LOGBOOK.md:706:- ARCHITECTURE: Resolution is synchronous on the caller for up to the configured timeout, while the concurrency test supplies its own goroutine. The relay I/O layer needs a bounded asynchronous resolver job/worker seam that reserves association ownership before lookup, invalidates completion by exact token, and returns all jobs and reservations to baseline on close, expiry, session loss, and cancellation.
LOGBOOK.md:708:- VERDICT/VERIFICATION: Routed to `to-dev`; evidence is `TASK-260715-3xpc6b_review.md`. Fresh pinned Go 1.26.5 repeat/race/full/vet/build/coverage/cross-compile gates, protocol check, 318 Swift tests/build, formatting, privacy scans, and board validation all pass, but they do not cover the rejected lifecycle paths.
LOGBOOK.md:711:- IMPLEMENTATION: Registry-owned nonblocking IPv4/IPv6 sockets now perform one `sendto` attempt per client datagram and bounded `recvmsg` turns. Replies preserve the kernel-observed source family, address, port, and payload bytes. `EAGAIN` returns a readiness-only retry result without idle activity or an internal retry buffer; `ENOBUFS`, oversize/truncation, terminal socket errors, closed/stale associations, and sink pressure map to finite wire errors or documented counted drops.
LOGBOOK.md:717:- REWORK: `EnsureFamilies` now reserves the complete canonical IPv4/IPv6 family set before opening either descriptor. A failure closes every staged descriptor and any socket already owned by that association exactly once, then restores association, socket, logical timer, event, and physical timer state to baseline. Lazy single-family admission remains available, but a failed later family completion also retires the partial association.
LOGBOOK.md:718:- RACE EVIDENCE: A private owner-loop seam submits an explicit obsolete timer arm after rearm and proves the arm counter increments without changing the live association or its one current logical/physical timer. Barrier-controlled tests repeat descriptor-use and activity races against local close, remote close, expiry, generation replacement, session close, session loss, process termination, and parent cancellation; each path proves its permitted linearization, stale token rejection or terminal owner closure, exactly-once descriptor close, and baseline restoration.
LOGBOOK.md:721:### 1527 — Relay UDP registry owns bounded unbound descriptors (TASK-260715-xw5dxc)
LOGBOOK.md:722:- IMPLEMENTATION: A single Go owner goroutine now linearizes relay association admission, descriptor use, activity, close, expiry, generation replacement, and cancellation. Identity is the session generation plus nonzero client association ID plus a monotonically increasing local incarnation, so stale timer/activity/close work cannot affect a reused numeric ID.
LOGBOOK.md:723:- RESOURCE POLICY: Association, family-specific socket, logical timer, and pending-close credits are checked before descriptor creation and report finite aggregate counters. One indexed deadline heap and one physical monotonic timer bound timer/task state; expiry events consume reserved pending-close credit until observed. IPv4 and IPv6 descriptors are created lazily and closed exactly once across normal, partial-failure, expiry, session, process, and cancellation paths.
LOGBOOK.md:724:- SOCKET POLICY: Production descriptors are standard-library UDP sockets configured nonblocking and close-on-exec. They are unconnected and deliberately unbound at registry creation; no public listener or privileged port is created. A later bounded `sendto` owner may allow the kernel to assign an ephemeral source port without changing registry ownership.
LOGBOOK.md:725:- VERIFICATION: Pinned Go 1.26.5 focused tests passed 20 repeated runs and the race detector; targeted lifecycle tests passed 100 repeated runs; full relay tests, vet, and build passed. `make relay-protocol-check` passed schema/vector/drift checks and 57 protocol tests; the full Swift suite passed 318 tests and Swift build passed. Real descriptor tests prove IPv4/IPv6 family, `O_NONBLOCK`, `FD_CLOEXEC`, port zero, unconnected state, and post-close `EBADF`; board, formatting, diff, privacy, and resource scans are clean.
LOGBOOK.md:734:- FAIL-CLOSED RULE: Missing, duplicate, wrong-phase, wrong-tuple, or accidentally active relay work makes the degraded predicate false and projects no usable service. T14/T15/T19/T22 establish the target lifecycle plus exact prospective registration before re-evaluation and publication; race-test handoff covers positive and negative cases.
LOGBOOK.md:740:- PROJECTION/DIAGRAMS: Public Full/Degraded predicates are target-state truth rather than circular source guards. Reasserting and every candidate/validator phase remain all-false. The lifecycle diagram now distinguishes M3 Awaiting Base from registered Validating Relay; the ownership view remains authority-only.
LOGBOOK.md:744:- GENERATION: Relay timers, attempts, sessions, health/process/channel watchers, and association callbacks now require a non-null M2 relay-work registration with a monotonic work generation, attempt generation when applicable, and finite phase. Retirement clears that registration before cleanup, publication, or rescheduling, so a late callback from attempt N is stale even before N+1 exists.
LOGBOOK.md:746:- ARTIFACTS/VALIDATION: The binding contract remains 11 states and 32 transitions and now contains 59 `M2CapabilityReasonV1` rows plus five retry classes. The lifecycle diagram exposes registered waiting/running reprobe phases; both PlantUML sources render deterministically and were visually inspected. Six main resources and all 16 downstream precondition copies byte-match. Privacy scan, 306 Swift tests, 57 RelayProtocol Swift tests plus schema checks, all Go relay packages, and 11 release-script tests pass. The zero-byte automatic raw spawn-log resource was removed.
LOGBOOK.md:751:- RETRY/REASON: The exhausted or local-change-required degraded path has no legal transition back to relay reprobe, and successful `connectingActivation` does not name an unambiguous finite published reason. The lifecycle diagram also needs separate waiting/running reprobe substates so it matches the binding transition table.
LOGBOOK.md:759:- ANOMALY/HANDOFF: Current M1 code uses one global schema-version equality check and does not yet force every non-usable lifecycle projection to all-false capabilities. Existing `TASK-260715-3edgwz` already owns the required kind-specific schema ranges and truthful M2 projection, so no duplicate task was created.
LOGBOOK.md:766:- VERIFICATION: Two complete matrix builds produced byte-identical pairs for all four relay and all four protocol-test executables. Go unit/race tests, vet, Python tests/compile, ShellCheck, manifest/checksum/license/SBOM verification, file/architecture/link inspection, existing Go/Swift protocol parity, 57 Swift protocol tests, schema regeneration, and Swift build pass. Darwin arm64 ran natively and Darwin amd64 under Rosetta 2; native Intel macOS and both native Linux rows remain mandatory release-CI gates and are not recorded as local passes.
LOGBOOK.md:778:- RELIABILITY/CLEANUP: Event traces now cover both IPv4→IPv6 and IPv6→IPv4 orders, cancellation/tombstone/late-response races, duplicate attempts before suppression, every M2 fallback trigger, malformed/mismatched no-shopping, same-endpoint TCP, later promotion, and exact attempt/terminal counts. Transactions, queue bytes, reservations, tombstones, connections, connection buffers, fixed bytes, and descriptors return to observed zero after every regenerated run.
LOGBOOK.md:782:- EVIDENCE/DECISION PROPOSED: Default policy is 4 endpoints, exact 65,535-byte DNS messages, 16 live owners, 256 KiB queued wire, 4 MiB aggregate DNS, and 2/5/30/8/10-second open/response/logical/startup/idle values; hard envelope is 8 endpoints, 32 owners, 1 MiB queue, 8 MiB aggregate, and 5/10/120/40/30 seconds. The full-wire message value is fixed rather than tunable so memory policy cannot truncate legitimate DNS/TCP responses.
LOGBOOK.md:784:- MEASUREMENT: Three main fixture runs plus three isolated default/hard memory runs passed IPv4/IPv6/dual/unreachable/malformed/stall/concurrent-failure/cancellation/M2 semantics, zero resolver calls, byte-for-byte allocation, zero FD delta, and zero tracked live bytes. Maximum isolated incremental physical footprint was 3,653,656/7,749,656 bytes. The controlled loopback results validate mechanics and bounds, not production SSH latency.
LOGBOOK.md:796:- DECISION: `TCPAdmissionRegistry` is one generation-scoped mutex authority for pending handshakes, authenticated flows, concurrent channel opens, owned channels, fixed directional queue capacity, live buffered bytes, and phase counts. All ceilings and the measured-safe/HEV evidence gates are caller-injected; denial is synchronous, retains no waiter, maps to SOCKS5 `REP=01`, and explicitly prohibits an SSH open. Reservation identifiers are monotonically allocated without reuse and fail closed with finite `identifier_capacity` pressure before `UInt64` wrap.
LOGBOOK.md:797:- OWNERSHIP: Handshake, flow, and opening tokens provide once-only release plus deinit rollback. Flow termination atomically releases an in-flight open, owned channel, reserved/live bytes, and phase slot before publishing one finite terminal reason. A late open completion after terminal cleanup is counted as a discarded event rather than a second release. Existing admitted flows continue byte/buffer progress while new work is rejected under pressure.
LOGBOOK.md:798:- DIAGNOSTICS: Runtime schema v1 is additively extended to 139 fixed counters, 75 fixed gauges, and two 12-bucket cumulative histograms. Registry-owned current gauges publish through a generation-scoped fixed-size atomic coalescer and reconcile into snapshots independently of the bounded event lane, so dropped counter/histogram ingestion remains observable without leaving phantom flow/open/channel/byte gauges. The TCP surface contains only aggregate pressure/terminal tokens, counts, bytes, health, and channel-open latency; it accepts no destination, hostname, address, port, payload, credential, flow/channel ID, or free-form label. Maximum populated encoding is 13,568 bytes of the 65,536-byte limit and generation advancement isolates stale publications.
LOGBOOK.md:799:- VERIFICATION: Eighteen admission tests cover below/at/above every independent ceiling, 128/256-way reserve races, 500 release/open and 500 late-callback races, 250 explicit-release/deinit ownership races, ID exhaustion, queued-byte overflow, session-health races, 8,000 sustained pressure rejects alongside 2,000 admitted-flow progress steps, every terminal reason across 320 churn lifecycles, and exact local/exported baseline recovery. The bounded-queue convergence probe passes 20/20 runs; focused normal and TSan admission/diagnostics suites pass; `make validate-core` passes boundary/native gates, 306 tests in 27 suites, and the post-test build; strict recursive Swift format, privacy/boundary, diff, and board validation pass.
LOGBOOK.md:803:- CANCELLATION: One locked control signal wakes local readiness, cancels both structured child tasks, calls `SSHByteChannel.cancel()` once, discards late positive completions, joins both directions, and releases the aggregate reservation once. EOF, cancellation, local/remote closure, read/write error, zero progress, and bound violation are handed to the later lifecycle owner as finite directional events; the pump does not choose half-close/reset policy.
LOGBOOK.md:804:- COVERAGE: Swift Testing exercises exact bidirectional hashes (`6d440a59…f5ad`, `66f02f2a…4378`), randomized fragmentation/partial writes/alternating local pressure, twelve changing seeds, SSH-credit suspension, fairness progress, all five suspending seams, late completion, typed failures, aggregate denial, no-spin permanent pressure, and a 100-run cleanup baseline.
LOGBOOK.md:808:- CORRECTION: The normative ownership sequence now matches `HEVSOCKSBoundary.swift:284-299`: immediately after TCP accept/socket setup, an already-stopped or generation-retired boundary or a full pending-authentication set closes the descriptor without a SOCKS5 method reply, RFC 1929 reply, authentication enqueue, or adapter handoff.
LOGBOOK.md:813:- DECISION: The shared iOS/macOS HEV boundary remains IPv4 loopback plus a fresh per-generation RFC 1929 capability; loopback/TCP peer metadata is explicitly not process identity. A descriptor reaches `SSHProxyAdapter` only after capability proof and is positioned at the first CONNECT-request byte.
LOGBOOK.md:814:- CORRECTION: Current `HEVSOCKSBoundary` uses `SO_RCVTIMEO`, which is a per-receive inactivity bound and can restart as bytes arrive; authentication sends have no deadline. The pending set is count-bounded, but current source does not prove an absolute accept-to-auth deadline. M1 must compute one monotonic deadline at accept, apply its remaining budget across greeting/auth reads, comparisons, and replies without progress extension, and prove slow-trickle/reply-stall eviction plus slot/descriptor release on iOS and macOS.
LOGBOOK.md:816:- RESOURCE: Adapter capacity is an atomic non-waiting reservation bounded by the HEV session ceiling and the measured M0/SSH/Core memory equation; exact flow, chunk, queue, window, and timeout values remain injected measurement inputs for M1/M3 rather than promoted constants. Diagnostics are aggregate schema-v1 only and exclude credentials, SOCKS bytes, endpoints, hostnames, payloads, and flow identifiers.
LOGBOOK.md:822:- COVERAGE: An injectable boundary matrix exercises the three public start errors plus an unknown exact domain/code independently through both Apple adapter modules. The real repository-to-controller disabled path asserts zero system start calls and zero manager mutations.
LOGBOOK.md:833:- PROJECTION: Every non-connected system status clears capability immediately. A connected projection accepts only correlated, negotiated lifecycle/capability responses at one matching position strictly newer than the last accepted generation/sequence; relaunch starts without historical app state and asks the provider again. Non-running provider lifecycle states fail safe to zero capabilities.
LOGBOOK.md:840:- COVERAGE: A deterministic two-platform matrix covers terminal-before-registration, terminal-during-registration without a notification, notification-before-token-return, notification-first completion, duplicate/late notification retirement, and cancellation. Every terminal/cancel path unregisters once and produces at most one completion.
LOGBOOK.md:868:### 0245 — VPN lifecycle separates ownership, enablement, system state, and runtime capability (TASK-260715-1q4qhw)
LOGBOOK.md:883:- FIX: The lifecycle now models one `generationConsumed` invariant shared by start and stop. A winning pre-start stop records termination, enters stopping, and runs the existing single cleanup owner; this releases the configuration reference before returning and makes every later start reject with `generationAlreadyConsumed`.
LOGBOOK.md:889:- FIX: After joining the startup child, `start()` now rechecks caller cancellation, environment cancellation, lifecycle ownership, and termination while actor-isolated before atomically clearing `startupTask` and returning. A callback that wins the handoff therefore forces the error/cleanup path; a callback that arrives after the atomic handoff observes no startup owner and starts cleanup itself.
LOGBOOK.md:907:- DECISION: `RuntimeDiagnosticsStore` owns one bounded generation with 96 counters, 56 gauges, one cumulative DNS-latency histogram, and at most one cataloged error per each of 10 finite domains. Packet/HEV use its `TunnelMetrics` recorder, SSH uses the same recorder without lane identity, and coordinator/TCP/DNS/route/memory use typed finite-category updates; unknown free-form metric names are counted then discarded without retaining the label.
LOGBOOK.md:909:- PRIVACY: The default schema has no lane/run/flow IDs, DNS or destination fields, addresses, credentials, commands/stdin, payload content, or traffic samples. State transition times are monotonic process-uptime milliseconds; errors retain only validated domain/code; flow, memory, route, health, packet/byte, drop-reason, DNS-result, and SSH values are aggregates.
LOGBOOK.md:916:- SAFETY: A future lifecycle `routeState` previously decoded as `unknown` while preserving true TCP, safe-DNS, route-installed, and health facts. Route state now participates in the shared fail-safe projection for both decoded and directly constructed lifecycle snapshots, forcing every capability false when it is unknown.
