LOGBOOK.md:1053:### 1349 — HEV 100-cycle descriptor-baseline check races cross-suite parallelism (review BUG-260720-2p4fln)
LOGBOOK.md:1055:- ROOT CAUSE: `openDescriptorCount()` counts process-global descriptors, but Swift Testing runs suites in parallel; flake log `.temp/BUG-260720-2p4fln/review/hev-flake-85.log` shows the fuzz suite still live when the 100-cycle test captured its baseline. Any concurrent suite's descriptor delta between baseline capture and final check breaks exact equality permanently; the 5s poll then times out. `.serialized` only guards within the HEV suite. Same fragile pattern at `HEVBridgeIntegrationTests.swift:230` (fault test, 3s).
LOGBOOK.md:1056:- DECISION: Review verdict — product changes accepted on evidence (stats snapshot, single boundary stop, quit-safety, TSan, lint, boundaries all green); task routed to-dev only to make the two descriptor-baseline assertions isolation-safe under full-package parallel runs and re-demonstrate repeatability.
LOGBOOK.md:1058:### 1339 — HEV statistics and teardown lifecycle repaired without changing upstream (BUG-260720-2p4fln)
LOGBOOK.md:1060:- ROOT CAUSE / DECISION: `boundary.stop()` was a product double invocation (`requestStop()` plus `waitForReturn()`), not a bad integration expectation. A lock-coalesced stop task now invokes the boundary once and lets both lifecycle paths await the same operation; unit and real-HEV cycle tests assert one start/one stop.
LOGBOOK.md:1062:- EXPECTATION FIX: A stalled SOCKS peer does not guarantee Darwin endpoint-B backpressure because native HEV may continue draining the tunnel descriptor. The stall test now asserts the deterministic contract (`sent + would-block drops + ENOBUFS drops == received`) and allows the legitimate zero-drop schedule instead of forcing timing-dependent loss.
LOGBOOK.md:1068:- EVIDENCE: `swift test --filter realHEVTCPMatrix` reaches the real SOCKS fixture and validates the full stream lifecycle, then fails only the `hev_transmitted_packets > 0` and `hev_received_packets > 0` assertions. Log: `.temp/TASK-260715-35wctc/swift-test-tcp-01.log`.
LOGBOOK.md:1081:### 1233 — PacketFlowBridge fault matrix closes the deferred contract rows (TASK-260715-3dn813)
LOGBOOK.md:1096:- ANOMALY: The broader pre-existing HEV Apple matrix stops before any Xcode build because `HevSocks5Tunnel.xcframework` contains macOS 10.14 and 11.0 object minima while its manifest requires only 10.14. This artifact is outside the libssh2 delta; the task-specific XCFramework inspection and required `make validate-core` gates pass.
LOGBOOK.md:1099:- BLOCKER: The accepted `libssh2_session_rekey` fork clears client byte/time/manual trigger initiation only. The exact public header exports no KEX lifecycle callback or generation query. Pinned libssh2 handles inbound `SSH_MSG_KEXINIT` entirely inside private `packet.c`/`transport.c` state, so the adapter cannot truthfully emit `.serverInitiated`, mark `activeKeyExchange`, increment generation, or distinguish server-KEX success/failure through the public boundary.
LOGBOOK.md:1102:- ROUTING: Do not report these gates green or build a mock-only partial adapter. Recommend a separately reviewed minimal fork extension exposing typed server-KEX lifecycle plus a nonblocking reply-correlated global-request/keepalive API; alternatives are explicitly failing the libssh2 candidate or revising the mandatory contract. Exact options and resume decision are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_server-rekey-keepalive-blocker.md`.
LOGBOOK.md:1123:- FINDING: Snapshot lifecycle is sticky-correct: `SSHConnectionStateMachine`'s own snapshot resets to nil on transition to `userAuthentication`/`active`, but `NIOSSHHandler` re-reads it after every processed message while the KEX state machine still holds `NegotiationResult`, and only overwrites on non-nil (`NIOSSHHandler.swift:684`). Consequence: during a rekey the handler snapshot updates when the new `NegotiationResult` is computed (KEXINIT processing), slightly before newKeys activate.
LOGBOOK.md:1162:- DECISION: Both engine adapters use one serialized, type-erased `ReluxTunnelCore` boundary with mandatory raw host-key policy evaluation before credential access, bounded partial writes, independent byte-stream halves, capped observable per-channel receive credit, automatic byte/time plus explicit/server rekey, keepalive, stable errors, schema-v1 metrics/events, and privacy-safe opaque lane identity. ADR-014 remains open; unsupported mandatory behavior is a red matrix row, never a candidate-specific skip.
LOGBOOK.md:1164:- VERIFICATION: The contract defines every operation, state, timeout/cancellation scope, error, metric, privacy rule, and E-ALGO/E-HOSTAUTH/E-CHANNELS/E-BACKPRESSURE/E-WINDOW/E-REKEY/E-KEEPALIVE/E-CANCEL/E-SCALE/E-ERRORS/E-METRICS-PRIVACY/E-INJECTION row. Three task-scoped PlantUML sources pass syntax checking and their lifecycle, pre-auth host verification, and channel/window/rekey SVG renders were visually inspected.
LOGBOOK.md:1174:- DECISION: ADR-014 remains open. Both candidates retain identical host-key, window, sustained 5 GiB rekey, scale (through 1,000 channels), cancellation, algorithm, extension-build, memory, and physical-size gates. Unknowns are named experiments in `.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md`; no missing capability was waived.
LOGBOOK.md:1179:- ROOT CAUSE: Any ordering where HEV main returns before `requestStop()` — init failure, or the contract-modeled "unexpected HEV main return while active" — makes `supervisedCleanup` (`Sources/ReluxTunnelCore/PacketFlowBridge.swift:1017`) invoke quit after fini: infinite 100 ms `usleep` loop on a cooperative executor thread; provider `stop()` never returns, descriptors never close. Plain `stop()` racing a just-died HEV hits the same path.
LOGBOOK.md:1180:- FINDING: Masked in tests because fake runtimes' `requestStop` never blocks and every lifecycle test stops HEV before its main returns.
